    weekArc: "Subsidiarity",
    weekArcLatin: "Subsidiaritas",
    title: "When the Higher Arrogates",
    scripture: {
      reference: "1 Samuel 8:11-14",
      text: "This will be the right of the king, that shall reign over you: He will take your sons, and put them in his chariots, and will make them his horsemen, and his running footmen to run before his chariots, And he will appoint of them to be his tribunes, and centurions, and to plough his fields, and to reap his corn, and to make him arms and chariots. Your daughters also he will take to make him ointments, and to be his cooks, and bakers. And he will take your fields, and your vineyards, and your best oliveyards, and give them to his servants.",
    },
    excerpt: {
      workId: "burke-reflections",
      author: "Edmund Burke",
      citation: "Reflections on the Revolution in France (1790)",
      text: "To be attached to the subdivision, to love the little platoon we belong to in society, is the first principle (the germ as it were) of public affections. It is the first link in the series by which we proceed towards a love to our country and to mankind. The interest of that portion of social arrangement is a trust in the hands of all those who compose it; and as none but bad men would justify it in abuse, none but traitors would barter it away for their own personal advantage.",
    },
    meditation:
      "Samuel warned them. They wanted a king like the other nations had. Samuel told them what a king does. He takes your sons. He takes your daughters. He takes your fields and your vineyards and your best oliveyards and gives them to his servants. The warning is not about a particular tyrant. It is about what scale does to a people. The higher you build the apparatus, the more it has to be fed, and what it eats is the local life of the people it claims to govern.\n\nThis is the dark version of what we considered yesterday. Pius XI gave us the principle in its calm doctrinal form. Samuel gives us its political shape. When the higher community arrogates to itself what the lower can do, the lower does not just lose function. It loses people. The sons go to the chariots. The daughters go to the ointments. The fields go to the king's servants. The village becomes a recruitment pool. The local court becomes a feeder for the federal docket. The parish becomes a subset of diocesan statistics.\n\nThe lawyer will recognize the pattern in the structure of modern federalization. There was a time when most of what affected most people's lives was handled by the county, the municipality, the state. Property, family, contracts, small criminal matters, the basic disputes of human life. The federal courts were for the things that genuinely required them, interstate matters, constitutional questions, treaties. Over the last sixty years that has shifted. More and more matters have been pulled upward. The list of federal crimes has grown into the thousands. The list of federal regulations into hundreds of thousands. A bar fight in a bus station can now be a federal case. A wetland on a farm can be regulated by an agency a thousand miles from the farm.\n\nThis is not a partisan complaint. Conservatives federalize when it suits them and liberals federalize when it suits them, and both will give you reasons why this particular matter really did require national handling. Sometimes the reasons are good. There are real things that need to be done at the national level: civil rights enforcement in jurisdictions that would otherwise refuse, antitrust against actors too large for any one state, banking regulation in a national market. The principle of subsidiarity does not deny this. What it denies is the presumption that higher is better, the reflex that when something is important it must therefore be handled high.\n\nBurke's phrase has become almost a cliché but the substance is exact. The little platoon. The subdivision. The first link in the series by which we proceed towards a love to our country and to mankind. Burke is not saying that the local is morally superior to the national. He is saying that the affections that make a national life possible are formed in the local. The man who has no parish has no nation. The lawyer who has no community has no commonwealth. To love your country, you must first have loved a place small enough to know.\n\nThe arrogation that Samuel warns about is not always dramatic. It is most often bureaucratic. A new statute passes. It creates federal jurisdiction over a category of matter that was formerly state. A new agency is established to administer it. The agency promulgates rules. The rules require reporting. The reporting requires staff. The staff develops expertise. The expertise becomes indispensable. Within a generation, the state-level institutions that used to handle the matter have lost the muscle. They no longer have the lawyers, the procedures, the institutional memory. The federal apparatus has not just taken the function; it has hollowed out the alternative. There is no longer a lower body that could resume the work even if the higher body wanted to give it back. This is what Samuel meant. The king takes the sons, and after a generation there are no sons left to do anything but be taken.\n\nFor the practicing lawyer this shows up at smaller scales every day. The state DOJ assigns itself a case the county DA was handling. Maybe the county DA was doing it poorly; maybe she was doing it well; the assumption is that state involvement improves things. Sometimes it does. Sometimes the state office moves slower than the county, misreads local conditions, alienates witnesses, loses the case the county would have won. The state attorneys are not bad. They are just farther from the life of the matter. They drive in from the capital, do their depositions, drive back. The county DA's office, meanwhile, learns the lesson that big cases will be taken from them, and stops developing the people who would have grown into them.\n\nThe parent firm does this to its affiliates. The national nonprofit does this to its chapters. The diocese does this to its parishes. The pattern is the same. A capacity is identified in the lower body. The higher body decides it could do better. The function is pulled upward. The lower body adjusts to its diminished role. The next time a similar matter arises, the lower body refers it up automatically. There is no longer anyone competent at that level. The hollowing is complete.\n\nNotice that Samuel does not say the people were wrong to want a king for the wrong reasons. He says they wanted a king at all. The desire itself was the disorder. A people that has stopped trusting its judges and its elders and its families to handle what they have always handled has already begun to die. The king only finishes the process.\n\nThe lawyer's question, then, is harder than it looks. It is not only: did the higher body act rightly when it took this matter? It is also: did we, at the lower level, invite the taking? Did we send the matter up because we could not be bothered, because we feared the responsibility, because we wanted someone else to decide? The arrogation of the higher and the abdication of the lower are usually two halves of the same fall.",
    prayer:
      "Lord, you warned your people what a king would do, and they wanted one anyway. Forgive us where we have wanted a higher authority because the lower responsibility frightened us. Give me the courage today to keep the matter where it belongs, and to bear what it costs to keep it there. Amen.",
    prompt:
      "Where in your work have you handed something upward because it was easier than carrying it yourself?",
